name: Coordinate Spaces
order: 3
tags: alert, anti-ballistix
------------------------------------------

There could be a lot of confusion about different coordinate reference frames we use in the game.
Especially when we translate coordinates between the screen <-> world ground <-> grid spaces. And sometimes, instead of absolute values we use quasi-normalized ones, which adds to confusion.


== Screen Coordinate Space

This coordinate space is defined by the actual browser viewport.

Absolute screen/canvas coordinates are used here,
originating from the top-left of the screen.

All mouse events originate in this coordinate system
and must be translated to the world, or grid, or other spaces
to be compatible with various entities.


== Backdrop/CityScape Coordinate Space

This space is used to define the sun, the stars, the skyline, and all other background elements.
It matches the screen coordinate space and uses the horizon line tuning property to properly split the sky and the ground.


== Arcade World (Port/Camera) Coordinate Space

The arcade world coordinate space is where all the action is going. All active entities live here. All collisions and explosions are happening in this space as well.

This coordinate space is defined by the city port camera that centers the x-origin at the middle of the screen, the y-origin is at the horizon line, and y-coordinates are flipped to make them Cartesian.

It always pans the width to match _env.playfield.width_ (2000 by default)
with height dynamically adjusted according to the current aspect ratio.
That makes the camera viewport pan from -1000 to 1000 horizontally.

The horizon line dynamically adjusts to the normalized value set in /env/tune every time the screen changes dimensions.

=== How to Orient Around

To help yourself to orient around the port/camera/world space, imagine the origin 0x0 to lie on the horizon line in the middle of the screen, at the intersection of the grid central line.
The positive X goes to the right up to +1000, the negative X goes to the left to -1000.
The positive Y goes up to whatever the current screen aspect ratio allows (relative to the fixed -1000 to 1000 x-axis base). And the negative Y goes down into the ground.

To simplify addressing in the arcade space, you can use:

* [crx(xP)](#./alt/crx) - translates a percentage value into the x-coordinate, where 0 is the left edge, and 100 is the right edge
* [cry(yP)](#./alt/cry) - translates a percentage value into the y-coordinate, where 0 is the bottom, and 100 is the top


== Sky Coordinate Space

* [lab/port/sky](#./lab/port/sky) - Sky Coordinate Ghost

The sky ```y = 0``` is considered to be on the horizon with Y values growing up and negatives going into the ground (exactly matching the world/port space). The X matches the world X.

