== Ground Coordinate Space

* [lab/port/ground](#./lab/port/ground) - Ground Coordinate Ghost

The ground y-origin is at the bottom of the camera view and the coordinate grows up. The X matches the world X.

=== Normalized Ground Coordinate Space



== Battleground Coordinate Space

The battlezone is defined by the active area of the ground where drones and missiles can hit, and it is a little bit narrower than the ground because of the exclusion zone at the bottom of the screen and far near the horizon.


=== Normalized Battlezone Coordinate Space



== Grid Coordinate Space

The grid is a quasi-3D entity constructed from Z-ordered rows of dots, all connected into a lattice.

=== Quasi-normalized viewport coordinates

=== 3D Grid

Each dot has actual 3D coordinates in the grid space projected into the 2D world view with Z-order properly adjusted. Since a horizontal row of dots will always have the same Z-depth relative to a fixed camera, we precalculate Z-order at the row level and render row-by-row.





=== Grid Dots

Each dot on the grid has [x, y, z] coordinates, where x defines the left-to-right position, y defines the height, and z defines the depth into the screen to the horizon.
The ranges for these values are roughly defined by the /env/playfield config, though some dots potentially can be out of range (and out of screen).

Each dot is connected to its peers to the left and right, and also to dots on other rows up and down the grid.
A dot is fixed horizontally (x) and in depth (z), but can oscillate up and down by changing its *y* coordinate.
The 0 value is the grid level and is considered to be a calm point for any dot. When y goes negative, the dot dives down and vice versa.

Dots under power stations are paired as lead-follower to sync their vertical movement (so a power station will never "ride" on a single dot - it always has to be on a double-dot hill).




